          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why does prelacteal feeding (giving honey, sugar water, or formula before the fi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why does prelacteal feeding (giving honey, sugar water, or formula before the first breastfeed) increase the risk of neonatal illness?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It causes severe allergic reactions in all newborns</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allergy is not the primary concern. The main risk is delayed lactation and early exposure of the immunologically naïve gut to environmental pathogens.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It delays milk let-down and exposes the newborn to pathogens</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Prelacteal feeds delay the establishment of breastfeeding and introduce microorganisms to the newborn's sterile gut, tripling the risk of neonatal diarrhoea.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It permanently stops breast milk production</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Prelacteal feeding delays lactation but does not permanently suppress it. The primary harm is early pathogen exposure and reduced colostrum intake.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It causes hepatotoxicity from sugar overload</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hepatotoxicity is not the mechanism. The harm lies in bypassing colostrum's immune protection and introducing environmental pathogens to the newborn gut.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Prelacteal feeds delay milk let-down and expose the immunologically naïve gut to environmental pathogens. This triples the risk of neonatal diarrhoea and deprives the baby of colostrum's protective antibodies."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Prelacteal feeds delay breastfeeding initiation and introduce pathogens, depriving the newborn of colostrum's immune protection at the most vulnerable time."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
